e line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nThus any line that is horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\) is **not** sunny.\n\nConsider the three horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\; y=2,\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\; y=3 .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct, each is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, and therefore none of them is sunny; consequently the number of sunny lines in the configuration is  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\nWe now verify that every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) lies on at least one of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\).\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Check | Lies on |\n|----------------|-------|----------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(b=1\\) | \\(\\ell_1\\) |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(b=1\\) | \\(\\ell_1\\) |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(b=1\\) | \\(\\ell_1\\) |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(b=2\\) | \\(\\ell_2\\) |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(b=2\\) | \\(\\ell_2\\) |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(b=3\\) | \\(\\ell_3\\) |\n\nAll six points are covered, satisfying the first requirement.  \nSince none of the three lines is sunny, the second requirement (exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines) is also satisfied.\n\nTherefore, for \\(n=3\\) there exists a configuration of three distinct lines meeting the problem’s conditions with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit ver