ny line can cover at most 3 points (one per column). So two sunny lines can cover at most 6 points (if they each cover one point in each column). So it's possible in principle.\n\nWe need to assign points to lines. Let's denote columns:\n\n- Column 1: points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\n- Column 2: points (2,1),(2,2)\n- Column 3: point (3,1)\n\nWe need to cover all these points with 3 lines, 2 of which are sunny (non-vertical, non-horizontal, non-slope -1). The third line can be any type (including vertical/horizontal/diagonal). Let's try to assign points to lines such that each column's points are covered.\n\nOne approach: Use vertical line x=1 to cover all three points in column 1. That uses one line (non-sunny). Then we need to cover column 2 (two points) and column 3 (one point) with two sunny lines. Since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, we need to cover column 2's two points with two distinct sunny lines, and column 3's point with one of those sunny lines (or the other). So we could have sunny line L2 covering (2,1) and (3,1) (one per column), and sunny line L3 covering (2,2) (and maybe something else). Let's see if there is a line that passes through (2,1) and (3,1). That's horizontal y=1, which is non-sunny. So not allowed. So we cannot have a sunny line covering both (2,1) and (3,1). So we need to assign each column's points to lines such that each sunny line covers at most one point per column. So we could have L2 covering (2,1) and maybe (1,2) (but (1,2) already covered by vertical line). But we can still have overlapping coverage; lines can cover points already covered by other lines. That's allowed. So we can have L2 covering (2,1) and (1,2). That would be a line through (2,1) and (1,2). The slope is (1-2)/(2-1) = -1/1 = -1, which is non-sunny. So not allowed.\n\nTry L2 covering (2,1) and (1,3): slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny (since slope -2 is not -1,0,∞). So L2: line through (1,3) and (2,1) has equation: slope -2, passing through (1,3): y - 3 = -2(x - 1) => y = -2x + 5. Indeed, that line also passes through (2,1) (check: y = -2*2 + 5 = 1). So L2 is sunny, covers (1,3) and (2,1). It also covers (3, -1) not needed. So L2 covers one point in column 1 (1,3) and one point in column 2 (2,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (2,2) and (3,1). Use L3 as a sunny line covering (2,2) and (3,1). The line through (2,2) and (3,1) has slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1/1 = -1, which is non-sunny. So not allowed. Try L3 covering (2,2) and (1,1): slope = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, which is sunny. So line through (1,1) and (2,2) is y = x. That's sunny (slope 1). It also covers (3,3) not needed. So L3 covers (1,1) and (2,2). However (1,1) is already covered by vertical line x=1, but that's okay. So L3 is sunny, covers (2,2) and (1,1). Now we have covered all points: column 1 points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3) covered by vertical line x=1 and also (1,1) and (1,3) covered by sunny lines. Column 2 points (2,1) covered by L2, (2,2) covered by L3. Column 3 point (3,1) is not yet covered. Wait, we haven't covered (3,1). L2 does not cover (3,1) because its line is y = -2x + 5; for x=3, y = -6 + 5 = -1, not 1. L3 is y = x, for x=3, y = 3, not 1. So (3,1) remains uncovered. We need to cover (3,1) with some line. We have used vertical line x=1, L2, L3. That's three lines already. We cannot add a fourth line. So this configuration fails.\n\nAlternative: Use vertical line x=3 to cover (3,1). Then we need to cover column 1 and column 2 with two sunny lines. Let's try vertical line x=3 (non-sunny). Then we need to cover column 1 (three points) and column 2 (two points) with two sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So with two sunny lines, we can cover at most two points in column 1 and two points in column 2. But column 1 has three points, column 2 has two points. So we cannot cover all points. However, we could have overlapping coverage: some points could be covered by the vertical line x=3? No, vertical line x=3 only covers column 3