imal covering because they would be less efficient than vertical lines? Let's think.\n\nWe need to cover all points in the triangular region with n lines. The region has n(n+1)/2 points. Each line can cover at most one point per column (if not vertical). So a non-vertical line can cover at most n points (one per column). A vertical line can cover multiple points (all points in its column). Horizontal line can cover at most one point per column as well, but also at most n points (since there are n columns). So vertical lines are the only lines that can cover more than one point per column. So to minimize the number of lines needed to cover all points, we would want to use as many vertical lines as possible because they cover many points. But we have a fixed number of lines n. So we need to allocate vertical lines and non-vertical lines to cover all points.\n\nIf we have v vertical lines, they cover sum_{a in V} (n+1-a) points, where V is the set of columns with vertical lines. The remaining points must be covered by non-vertical lines, each covering at most one point per column. So the total number of points that can be covered by non-vertical lines is at most (n - v) * (n - v) (since each non-vertical line can cover at most one point per column, and there are n - v columns without vertical lines). Actually each non-vertical line can cover at most one point per column, but there are n columns total. However, if a column has a vertical line, the points in that column are already covered, so we don't need non-vertical lines to cover them. So the non-vertical lines need to cover points in columns without vertical lines. There are n - v such columns. In each such column, there are (n+1-a) points. The total number of points to be covered by non-vertical lines is S = sum_{a not in V} (n+1-a). If we choose V to be the first v columns (largest columns), then S = (n-v)(n-v+1)/2. That's the minimal possible S, because any other choice of V would leave columns with larger sizes