"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have a complete solution. The admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Two easy constructions.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=2x-(2n-1),\n   \\]\n   which contains the unique point \\((n,1)\\).\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=n\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=i\\,x-(i^2+i-1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct.  \n   For a fixed column \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,y_i(a)=i\\,a-(i^2+i-1)\\mid i=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   equals \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\}\\).  Consequently each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=0}^{n-1}\\) cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, where \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   * \\(v\\) = number of vertical lines,  \n   * \\(h\\) = number of horizontal lines,  \n   * \\(d\\) = number of lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   v+h+d+k=n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad t:=n-v=h+d+k\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   is the number of *non\u2011vertical* lines.  \n   The columns that do **not** contain a vertical line are exactly \\(t\\) columns; we may assume (by re\u2011labelling the axes) that they are the rightmost ones  \n\n   \\[\n   C_{n-t+1},C_{n-t+2},\\dots ,C_n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Column \\(C_{n-t+i}\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le t\\)) contains \\(i\\) points, so the total number of points that must be covered by the \\(t\\) non\u2011vertical lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\sum_{i=1}^{t} i=\\frac{t(t+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Every non\u2011vertical line meets each of the \\(t\\) columns in **at most one** point; therefore the \\(t\\) non\u2011vertical lines can cover at most \\(t\\cdot t=t^{2}\\) points.  Hence we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   t^{2}\\ge S=\\frac{t(t+1)}2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;2t\\ge t+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;t\\ge1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality in (5) forces each non\u2011vertical line to meet **every** one of the \\(t\\) columns (otherwise the total number of covered points would be \\(<S\\)).  Consequently each of the \\(t\\) non\u2011vertical lines contains exactly one point of each column \\(C_{n-t+1},\\dots ,C_n\\).\n\n   Consider the right\u2011most column \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\).  \n   Because there is no vertical line \\(x=n\\) (otherwise \\(v=n\\) and \\(k=0\\)), the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on a non\u2011vertical line \\(L\\).  By the observation above, \\(L\\) must also contain a point of every other column \\(C_{n-t+1},\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\).\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be the point of \\(L\\) in column \\(C_{n-1}\\) (\\(a=n-1\\)).  \n   Since \\(L\\) contains \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-1,b)\\), its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{b-1}{(n-1)-n}=\\frac{1-b}{1}=1-b .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L\\) is *sunny*, so \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence \\(b\\neq1\\) (otherwise \\(m=0\\)) and \\(b\\neq2\\) (otherwise \\(m=-1\\)).  Because column \\(C_{n-1}\\) contains exactly the two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\), the only possibilities for \\(b\\) are \\(1\\) or \\(2\\).  Both are excluded, a contradiction.\n\n   Therefore a configuration with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) cannot exist.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions exhibit that \\(k=0,1,n\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\); the impossibility argument rules out all other integers.  Hence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,n\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\)."
